// │ ⚠️  DO NOT RESTORE A SOC 2 DATE OR STATUS IN THE COMPLIANCE SECTION.      │
// │                                                                           │
// │ The public SOC 2 Type I claim was withdrawn on 2026-08-30 by founder      │
// │ decision, because no auditor was engaged behind the Q3 2026 target it     │
// │ stated. A status ("in progress") or a quarter is a commitment nothing     │
// │ backs, and the section immediately above this one is a list of security   │
// │ claims we can each point at a test for — this is the one that had none.   │
// │                                                                           │
// │ The readiness programme is NOT cancelled. It is parked, deliberately, and │
// │ the roadmap now lives in datanika-core at                                 │
// │ docs/specs/SPEC_SOC2_ROADMAP.md (moved out of plans/ 2026-08-31, core     │
// │ #734). Nothing here should be read, or rewritten, as abandoning it — only │
// │ as declining to publish a date.                                           │
// │                                                                           │
// │ `tests/compliance-claims.test.ts` sweeps EVERY file under src/, not just  │
// │ this page: on 2026-08-30 the claim was living in four files across four   │
// │ pages, including a meta description and a four-month-old blog post.       │
// │                                                                           │
// │ ⚠️  Keep notes like this one in the frontmatter. Astro strips these; an   │
// │ `<!-- … -->` in the template below is SERVED to every visitor. A warning  │
// │ candid enough to be useful is exactly the kind you do not want shipped.   │
// └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Navbar from "../components/Navbar.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Trust & Security — Datanika"
  description="Datanika's security architecture, compliance posture, data protection practices, and infrastructure overview. Hosted in the EU, open-source and auditable, every sub-processor named."
>
  <Navbar />
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 py-20">
    <h1 class="mb-2 text-4xl font-bold gradient-text">Trust & Security</h1>
    <p class="mb-10 text-lg text-slate-400">
      How we protect your data, your credentials, and your pipelines.
    </p>

    <div class="space-y-12 text-slate-300 leading-relaxed [&_h2]:text-2xl [&_h2]:font-semibold [&_h2]:text-white [&_h2]:mb-4 [&_h3]:text-lg [&_h3]:font-medium [&_h3]:text-white [&_h3]:mb-2 [&_ul]:list-disc [&_ul]:pl-6 [&_ul]:space-y-2">

      <!-- Security Architecture -->
      <section>
        <h2>Security Architecture</h2>
        <p class="mb-4">
          Security is built into every layer of Datanika — from how we store credentials
          to how we isolate tenants. Our codebase is open-source, so every security claim
          below is verifiable in code.
        </p>

        <div class="grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/50 p-5">
            <h3>Multi-tenant isolation</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">
              All tables use <code class="text-violet-400">org_id</code> column filtering
              via <code class="text-violet-400">TenantMixin</code>. Every query is scoped
              to the authenticated organization. A dedicated
              <strong class="text-slate-300">cross-tenant isolation suite</strong> verifies
              that Org A cannot read, update, or delete Org B's data across connections,
              uploads, runs, and notifications, with additional
              <code class="text-violet-400">wrong_org</code> coverage in every service
              test module.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/50 p-5">
            <h3>Credential encryption</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">
              Connection credentials (API keys, database passwords, service account JSON)
              are encrypted at rest using <strong class="text-slate-300">Fernet</strong>
              symmetric encryption. Credentials are decrypted only at pipeline runtime,
              never logged, and never exposed in the UI after initial save.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/50 p-5">
            <h3>Authentication</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">
              Passwords are hashed with <strong class="text-slate-300">bcrypt</strong>.
              Sessions use <strong class="text-slate-300">JWT</strong> tokens. API access
              uses scoped API keys. SSO is available via <strong class="text-slate-300">SAML 2.0</strong>
              and <strong class="text-slate-300">OIDC</strong> on the Enterprise plan.
              OAuth login with Google and GitHub is available on all plans.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/50 p-5">
            <h3>Authorization</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">
              Role-based access control with four roles: <strong class="text-slate-300">Owner</strong>,
              <strong class="text-slate-300">Admin</strong>,
              <strong class="text-slate-300">Editor</strong>, and
              <strong class="text-slate-300">Viewer</strong>. Permissions are enforced at
              both the UI state layer and the service layer — no client-side-only guards.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/50 p-5">
            <h3>Audit logging</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">
              All mutations (create, update, delete) are logged with user ID, organization,
              action type, and timestamp. Audit logs are queryable per-org and retained
              for the lifetime of the account.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/50 p-5">
            <h3>Security testing</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">
              <strong class="text-slate-300">112 security test functions</strong> across
              11 dedicated test files covering authentication, injection, path traversal,
              tenant isolation, rate limiting, token security, file-upload security, CSRF,
              input validation, and API-key security. Every push to <code class="text-violet-400">dev</code>
              runs the full suite. Count taken 2026-08-30 — the suite grows, so
              <a href="https://github.com/datanika-io/datanika-core/tree/master/tests/test_security" class="text-violet-400 hover:underline">count them yourself</a>
              rather than take ours.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Compliance -->
      <section>
        <h2>Compliance</h2>

        <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/50 p-6 mb-6">
          <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
            <div class="flex-shrink-0 mt-1">
              <svg class="h-6 w-6 text-slate-400" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z" />
              </svg>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-1">SOC 2 — no attestation, and no date</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">
                We hold no SOC 2 attestation, Type I or Type II, and we are not publishing
                a target date for one. Until 2026-08-30 this page carried a SOC 2 Type I
                commitment with a stated completion target of
                <strong class="text-slate-300">Q3 2026</strong>. No auditor had been engaged
                behind that date. We withdrew it rather than let it stand, and we are
                recording the withdrawal here rather than quietly deleting the sentence —
                anyone who read the earlier page should be able to see what changed.
                If an attestation is a hard requirement for your procurement process today,
                we are not the right vendor for you yet, and we would rather say so now than
                at the end of a security review.
              </p>
              <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 mt-2">
                What we can point at instead is checkable without taking our word for it.
                The security test suite described above runs on every push. The core is
                open source, so every claim on this page can be read in the code that
                implements it — including the ones that are inconvenient for us. Every
                sub-processor is named below, with what it receives and where it sits,
                and the change log records when that list was wrong. And when we fix a
                security issue we say so in the release notes rather than shipping it
                quietly; our disclosure policy is in
                <a href="https://github.com/datanika-io/datanika-core/blob/master/SECURITY.md" class="text-violet-400 hover:underline">SECURITY.md</a>.
              </p>
              <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 mt-2">
                Evaluating Datanika and need to go further than this page — a security
                questionnaire, a DPA, or a walkthrough of a specific control? Email
                <a href="mailto:security@datanika.io" class="text-violet-400 hover:underline">security@datanika.io</a>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">GDPR:</strong> the application, its database and its
            off-site backups are all in Greece, in the EU. Data Processing Agreement (DPA)
            available on request — email
            <a href="mailto:info@datanika.io" class="text-violet-400 hover:underline">info@datanika.io</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">Data residency:</strong> your account data and your
            pipeline data are stored in Greece (EU) and backed up to a second Greek provider.
            Three sub-processors operate outside the EU and receive only what their function
            needs — <strong class="text-slate-300">Resend</strong> (US) receives the recipient
            address on transactional email, <strong class="text-slate-300">Cloudflare</strong>
            (US, global points of presence) terminates TLS and so sees IP addresses and request
            metadata, and <strong class="text-slate-300">Paddle</strong> (UK) holds billing
            details. Those transfers rely on the Standard Contractual Clauses in each
            provider's DPA, on their EU–U.S. Data Privacy Framework certifications, and on the
            UK adequacy decision. Data also leaves the EU when you configure it to — pointing a
            pipeline at a US-region BigQuery destination, for example. Full breakdown in
            <a href="/privacy" class="text-violet-400 hover:underline">the privacy policy</a>,
            section 9.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">Data retention and deletion:</strong> pipeline run
            history, run logs and configuration metadata are retained for as long as the
            organization exists — we do not currently run a time-based purge of run logs.
            Organizations and connections use a soft-delete model: records are marked deleted
            and removed from all application code paths, but the underlying Fernet-encrypted
            data remains in the database until a hard delete is run. There is no self-service
            delete button in the product yet. Hard deletion of an organization and all
            associated data is done by us on request — email
            <a href="mailto:security@datanika.io" class="text-violet-400 hover:underline">security@datanika.io</a>,
            and personal data is removed within 30 days, including from the off-site backups,
            whose retention window is exactly 30 days.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Infrastructure -->
      <section>
        <h2>Infrastructure</h2>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800">
                <th class="text-left py-3 pr-4 text-slate-400 font-medium">Layer</th>
                <th class="text-left py-3 pr-4 text-slate-400 font-medium">Provider</th>
                <th class="text-left py-3 text-slate-400 font-medium">Details</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-slate-300">
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">Application hosting</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Pointer (pointer.gr)</td>
                <td class="py-3">Virtual private server in Greece (EU). Ubuntu 22.04 LTS, 4 vCPU / 8 GB.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">Off-site backups</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Aweb</td>
                <td class="py-3">Second provider, also in Greece (EU). Nightly database dump, 30-day retention.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">CDN & DDoS protection</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Cloudflare</td>
                <td class="py-3">All traffic proxied. WAF rules, rate limiting, bot management.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">TLS</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Cloudflare + Apache</td>
                <td class="py-3">
                  TLS 1.2+ enforced. Cloudflare Origin Certificate for the backend. HSTS enabled.
                  Apache 2.4 is the reverse proxy on the application server; this marketing site is
                  served by nginx.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">Database</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">PostgreSQL 16</td>
                <td class="py-3">Encrypted connections. Nightly automated backups, kept 7 days locally and 30 days off-site.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">Transactional email</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Resend</td>
                <td class="py-3">Password resets and team invitations, over SMTP with TLS. United States.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">Monitoring</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Prometheus + Grafana</td>
                <td class="py-3">Self-hosted on the application server. Container metrics, resource utilization, alerting.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">Analytics</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Plausible CE + Cloudflare Web Analytics</td>
                <td class="py-3">
                  Both are cookie-free: no cookies, no browser storage, no cross-site identifier,
                  no advertising or behavioural profile. Plausible CE is self-hosted on our own
                  Greek server. Cloudflare Web Analytics runs on this marketing site only, for
                  page-load timings. Cloudflare already terminates TLS for every request you make
                  to us, so this is an additional function performed by a sub-processor we already
                  use — not a new company receiving your data.
                </td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Subprocessors -->
      <section id="subprocessors">
        <h2>Subprocessors</h2>
        <p class="mb-4">
          The following third-party services process data on behalf of Datanika customers:
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800">
                <th class="text-left py-3 pr-4 text-slate-400 font-medium">Subprocessor</th>
                <th class="text-left py-3 pr-4 text-slate-400 font-medium">Purpose</th>
                <th class="text-left py-3 text-slate-400 font-medium">Data location</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-slate-300">
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">
                  Pointer (pointer.gr)
                  <span class="block font-normal text-slate-500 text-xs mt-1">
                    T. Papamichail – Vainas / G. Psaltakis O.E. · VAT EL998633174
                  </span>
                </td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Application hosting, database, worker processes</td>
                <td class="py-3">Greece, EU</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">Aweb</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Off-site database backups (a full copy, 30-day retention); hosting for datanika.io and our self-hosted analytics</td>
                <td class="py-3">Greece, EU</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">Cloudflare, Inc.</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">
                  CDN, DDoS protection, DNS, TLS termination; cookie-free web analytics on
                  datanika.io; inbound routing of mail sent to our published addresses
                </td>
                <td class="py-3">United States; served from the nearest point of presence</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">Resend (Plus Five Five, Inc.)</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Transactional email delivery — password resets, team invitations</td>
                <td class="py-3">United States</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800/50">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">Paddle.com Market Ltd</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Payment processing (Merchant of Record)</td>
                <td class="py-3">United Kingdom</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-white">GitHub (Microsoft)</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4">Source code hosting and CI/CD. Holds our code, not customer data.</td>
                <td class="py-3">United States</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-500">
          We notify customers of subprocessor changes at least 30 days in advance.
          To receive notifications, email
          <a href="mailto:info@datanika.io" class="text-violet-400 hover:underline">info@datanika.io</a>
          with subject "Subprocessor updates."
        </p>

        <div class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/50 p-5">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-white mb-2">Change log</h3>
          <ul class="text-sm text-slate-400 space-y-1">
            <li>
              <strong class="text-slate-300">2026-09-04</strong> — no company was added or removed.
              <strong class="text-slate-300">Cloudflare</strong>'s entry gained a function: it also
              provides the cookie-free web analytics on datanika.io described in the Analytics row
              above. That measurement had been running without being named here, which is why this
              is recorded as a correction rather than left as a silent edit. The Analytics row was
              also made more precise — it previously read "No third-party tracking", which was
              true of cookies and cross-site identifiers and not of the fact that a second
              measurement existed.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-slate-300">2026-08-30</strong> — this list was corrected. Our
              production host changed on 2026-07-17 and the change was not reflected here at the
              time; we are recording it now rather than quietly editing the table. Added:
              <strong class="text-slate-300">Pointer</strong> (application hosting, replacing
              Hetzner), <strong class="text-slate-300">Aweb</strong> (off-site backups, previously
              unlisted), and <strong class="text-slate-300">Resend</strong> (transactional email,
              replacing the Google Workspace entry, which never handled it).
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-slate-300">2026-07-17</strong> — application hosting moved from
              Hetzner (Germany) to Pointer (Greece). Both are in the EU.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Open Source -->
      <section>
        <h2>Open Source</h2>
        <p>
          Datanika's core is open-source at
          <a href="https://github.com/datanika-io/datanika-core" class="text-violet-400 hover:underline">github.com/datanika-io/datanika-core</a>.
          This means:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">Auditability:</strong> every security claim on this page
            is verifiable in the source code. The security test suite, the
            <code class="text-violet-400">TenantMixin</code>, the Fernet encryption layer —
            all public.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">No vendor lock-in:</strong> you can self-host Datanika
            on your own infrastructure with full control over data residency and network
            access. See the <a href="/docs/self-hosting" class="text-violet-400 hover:underline">self-hosting guide</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">Community review:</strong> security-critical code paths
            benefit from community scrutiny in addition to our internal testing.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Responsible Disclosure -->
      <section>
        <h2>Responsible Disclosure</h2>
        <p class="mb-4">
          We take security vulnerabilities seriously. If you discover a vulnerability
          in Datanika, please report it responsibly:
        </p>

        <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/50 p-6">
          <p class="text-white font-medium mb-3">Report a vulnerability</p>
          <p class="mb-3">
            Email
            <a href="mailto:security@datanika.io" class="text-violet-400 hover:underline font-medium">security@datanika.io</a>
            with a description, steps to reproduce, and affected components.
          </p>
          <div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3 text-sm">
            <div class="rounded border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900 p-3 text-center">
              <p class="text-slate-400">Acknowledgement</p>
              <p class="text-white font-semibold mt-1">48 hours</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900 p-3 text-center">
              <p class="text-slate-400">Initial assessment</p>
              <p class="text-white font-semibold mt-1">7 days</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900 p-3 text-center">
              <p class="text-slate-400">Patch release</p>
              <p class="text-white font-semibold mt-1">90 days</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mt-4">
            Full disclosure policy:
            <a href="https://github.com/datanika-io/datanika-core/blob/master/SECURITY.md" class="text-violet-400 hover:underline">SECURITY.md</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Contact -->
      <section>
        <h2>Questions?</h2>
        <p>
          For security questions, vulnerability reports, or DPA requests:
          <a href="mailto:security@datanika.io" class="text-violet-400 hover:underline">security@datanika.io</a>
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          For general compliance inquiries and subprocessor update subscriptions:
          <a href="mailto:info@datanika.io" class="text-violet-400 hover:underline">info@datanika.io</a>
        </p>
      </section>

    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
